<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Tetrarchy of Philip</h1><p class="st-meta">4 BCE – 34 CE · capital Caesarea Philippi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The north-eastern share of Herod the Great's kingdom, ruled for almost four decades by his son Philip from the new city of Caesarea Philippi.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-the-great">Herod the Great</a> died in 4 BCE, Augustus confirmed his will with changes and divided the kingdom among three sons. Philip received the lands north and east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-galilee">Sea of Galilee</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaulanitis">Gaulanitis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batanaea">Batanaea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trachonitis">Trachonitis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hauran">Auranitis</a> and part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ituraea">Ituraea</a>, a frontier zone the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> had entrusted to Herod to suppress brigandage.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Philip governed as a Roman client with the title tetrarch. His subjects were largely Syrian, Arab and Greek-speaking pagans with Jewish communities, and he was the first Jewish ruler to put the emperor's portrait on his coins. He refounded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banias">Paneas</a> at the springs of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hashemite-kingdom-of-jordan">Jordan</a> as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-caesarea">Caesarea</a> Philippi and raised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethsaida">Bethsaida</a> to a city named Julias. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> describes him as a mild, accessible ruler who travelled his territory to judge cases.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Philip died in 34 CE without children, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> attached the tetrarchy to the province of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a>. In 37 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caligula">Caligula</a> granted it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-agrippa-i">Herod Agrippa I</a>, making it the core of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-herod-agrippa">Kingdom of Herod Agrippa</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banias">Caesarea Philippi</a> remains known from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> as the place of Peter's confession.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 4 BCE – 34 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:59.62%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>4 BCE – 34 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Caesarea Philippi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Philip the Tetrarch</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greco-Roman polytheism, Judaism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Aramaic, Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>, <a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tetrarchy of Batanaea, Ituraea and Trachonitis</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=15&amp;focus=tetrarchy-of-philip" aria-label="Tetrarchy of Philip on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5593 971 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tetrarchy of Philip lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5913 12